{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis component applies the WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern to page-level route navigation \u2014 a fundamental semantic misapplication. Every ARIA attribute is technically present, but the pattern is the wrong one for the interaction model. The result is a component that passes a superficial ARIA checklist while breaking multiple screen reader navigation strategies, losing focus after every route change, and presenting navigation items as buttons instead of links. The \"Accessibility Features Present\" checklist in the design rationale is accurate about what's in the DOM but misleading about whether those features serve the user.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted for a \"tabbed navigation using the Tab pattern for page-level routes\":\n\n1. **Wrong ARIA pattern** \u2014 Tabs are for in-page view switching, not route navigation. \u2705 Confirmed.\n2. **Broken `aria-controls`** \u2014 Only one panel exists in the DOM; the other three references dangle. \u2705 Confirmed.\n3. **Focus loss after route change** \u2014 `router.push()` triggers a re-render with no focus management. \u2705 Confirmed.\n4. **Roving tabindex on navigation items** \u2014 Only the active tab is in the tab order; others require arrow keys. \u2705 Confirmed.\n5. **`role=\"tabpanel\"` wrapping full page content** \u2014 Semantically wrong; the content is a page, not a panel. \u2705 Confirmed.\n\nAll five predictions were confirmed. Two additional issues surfaced: no `<nav>` landmark, and `aria-selected` used where `aria-current=\"page\"` is correct.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n### C1. WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern misapplied to page-level navigation\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` \u2014 the entire component. `router.push(sections[newIndex].path)` (line 22, 27, 32, 35) triggers route changes with distinct URLs. The \"Expected Behavior\" section confirms: *\"Browser back/forward works (each tab is a route change).\"*\n\n**The problem:** The WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern is defined for *\"a set of parallel layers of content, where each layer contains a single scrollable view\"* \u2014 in-page view switching. It is explicitly **not** for navigation between distinct pages. The differentiator is:\n\n| | Tabs (APG) | Navigation (this component) |\n|---|---|---|\n| URL | Same (or hash) | Different per section |\n| Browser history | No entry | Entry per section |\n| Back/forward | N/A | Works |\n| Content lifecycle | Panel swap in-place | Route change, full re-render |\n\nThis component exhibits navigation behavior (distinct URLs, history entries, back/forward) but uses the tab semantic model. This is not a partial implementation \u2014 it is the wrong pattern.\n\n**User impact:**\n- **Screen reader users navigating by links** (JAWS `K`, NVDA `H`/`L`): These items are `<button>` elements with `role=\"tab\"`. They will **not** appear in link navigation. A screen reader user who has memorized \"press K to jump to the next link\" will skip this entire navigation.\n- **Screen reader users navigating by landmarks** (JAWS `B`, NVDA `D`): The container is a `<div>`, not a `<nav>`. This navigation is **invisible** to landmark navigation.\n- **Screen reader users navigating by tabs** (JAWS `G`, NVDA `T`): They will find the tablist, but the interaction model is wrong. Tabs don't trigger page loads. The user expects a panel swap; they get a route change with focus loss.\n- **All screen reader users:** The `aria-controls` references are broken (see C2), so the tab\u2192panel relationship is semantically incoherent.\n\n**WCAG/APG citation:** WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern \u2014 *\"A tab is a type of button that controls a tab panel. Tabs are used to switch between views within a single page.\"* WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 the semantic structure does not match the actual relationship (navigation, not tab control).\n\n**Fix:** Replace the Tabs pattern with navigation semantics:\n\n```jsx\n<nav aria-label=\"Product sections\">\n  <ul className=\"product-nav-list\">\n    {sections.map((section) => (\n      <li key={section.id}>\n        <a\n          href={section.path}\n          aria-current={pathname.startsWith(section.path) ? 'page' : undefined}\n          className={`product-nav-link ${pathname.startsWith(section.path) ? 'active' : ''}`}\n        >\n          {section.label}\n        </a>\n      </li>\n    ))}\n  </ul>\n</nav>\n```\n\nRemove `role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, `role=\"tabpanel\"`, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls`, `aria-labelledby`, roving tabindex, and arrow key handlers. The content area is simply the page content \u2014 no `role=\"tabpanel\"` wrapper.\n\nIf arrow key navigation is desired as an enhancement, it can be added on top of the `<a>` elements, but the base semantics must be navigation, not tabs.\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** This is not a missing attribute or a minor inconsistency. The entire semantic model is wrong. Screen reader users who rely on link navigation or landmark navigation \u2014 the two most common navigation strategies \u2014 cannot find or correctly interact with this component. The \"Accessibility Features Present\" checklist is a false positive: all the ARIA attributes are there, but they describe a pattern that doesn't match the interaction.\n\n---\n\n### C2. Three of four `aria-controls` references point to non-existent elements\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` line 44: `aria-controls={`panel-${section.id}`}`. The `role=\"tabpanel\"` div (line 57) has `id={`panel-${sections[activeIndex >= 0 ? activeIndex : 0].id}`}` \u2014 only **one** panel exists in the DOM at any time.\n\nWhen the user is on `/product/overview`:\n- `aria-controls=\"panel-overview\"` \u2192 \u2705 exists\n- `aria-controls=\"panel-specs\"` \u2192 \u274c **does not exist**\n- `aria-controls=\"panel-reviews\"` \u2192 \u274c **does not exist**\n- `aria-controls=\"panel-support\"` \u2192 \u274c **does not exist**\n\n**User impact:**\n- **Screen reader users:** When a screen reader user activates a tab, the AT attempts to move focus to the referenced panel. Three of four tabs reference elements that don't exist. The AT either ignores the reference or logs an error. The tab\u2192panel relationship is semantically broken.\n- **All AT users:** The `aria-controls` / `aria-labelledby` pairing is the core relationship in the Tabs pattern. With 75% of the references dangling, the pattern is incoherent.\n\n**WCAG/APG citation:** WAI-ARIA spec \u2014 `aria-controls` must reference an element that exists in the accessibility tree. WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) \u2014 the relationship between tab and panel must be programmatically determinable.\n\n**Fix:** This finding is resolved by the C1 fix (removing the Tabs pattern entirely). If the Tabs pattern were correct for this use case, the fix would be to render all four panels in the DOM and toggle visibility with `hidden` or `display: none`. But since the pattern is wrong, the correct fix is to remove `aria-controls` entirely.\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** Broken ARIA references are worse than missing ARIA. A missing attribute is a gap; a broken reference is a lie. Screen readers that attempt to resolve `aria-controls` to a non-existent element may behave unpredictably.\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n### M1. Navigation items are `<button>` elements, not `<a>` links\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` line 42: `<button role=\"tab\" ...>`. Each navigation item is a button that calls `router.push()`.\n\n**User impact:**\n- **All users:** Buttons do not support middle-click (open in new tab), right-click \u2192 \"Open link in new tab,\" Ctrl+click, or drag-to-bookmark. These are standard navigation interactions that users expect.\n- **Screen reader users:** Link navigation (JAWS `K`, NVDA `L`) will not find these items. They are buttons, not links.\n- **Keyboard users:** `Enter` activates a button (correct), but the semantic expectation for navigation items is a link.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 the element type should match the semantic role. Navigation items are links, not buttons.\n\n**Fix:** Use `<a href={section.path}>` instead of `<button>`. Next.js's `useRouter().push()` is unnecessary when using `<a>` \u2014 the browser handles navigation natively, and Next.js intercepts the click for client-side routing automatically.\n\n```jsx\n<a href={section.path} aria-current={isActive ? 'page' : undefined}>\n  {section.label}\n</a>\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** This is a functional and semantic gap. Users lose standard link interactions. Screen reader users lose link-based navigation. The fix is straightforward: replace `<button>` with `<a>`.\n\n---\n\n### M2. No focus management after route change\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` \u2014 `router.push(sections[newIndex].path)` (lines 22, 27, 32, 35) and `onClick={() => router.push(section.path)}` (line 47). No `useEffect`, no `setTimeout`, no focus management code exists anywhere in the component.\n\n**The problem:** When `router.push()` triggers a client-side route change:\n1. The old tab element (which had focus) is unmounted.\n2. The new page content renders.\n3. **Focus is lost.** The browser does not automatically move focus to the new content.\n4. The user is stranded \u2014 focus is on `<body>` or nowhere.\n\nFor a keyboard user: they press `ArrowRight` on the \"Overview\" tab, the route changes to \"Specifications,\" and focus vanishes. They must Tab from the top of the page to get back to the navigation.\n\nFor a screen reader user: the content changes, but there is no announcement and no focus movement. The user doesn't know the content has changed.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order) \u2014 focus must be in a logical order. WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages) \u2014 dynamic content changes must be communicated. SPA accessibility guidance \u2014 focus must be managed on client-side route changes.\n\n**Fix:** Add focus management after route change:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  // Move focus to the new page's main heading or content area\n  const mainContent = document.querySelector('.product-content h1, .product-content h2, .product-content');\n  if (mainContent) {\n    mainContent.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');\n    setTimeout(() => mainContent.focus({ preventScroll: true }), 0);\n  }\n}, [pathname]);\n```\n\nOr, if the page content has a heading, focus that heading. The key principle: after a route change, focus must move to the new content so the user knows where they are.\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** Every single navigation action (click or arrow key) results in focus loss. This is not an edge case \u2014 it is the primary interaction. A keyboard user who navigates to \"Specifications\" via arrow key is immediately stranded.\n\n---\n\n### M3. Roving tabindex prevents Tab access to inactive navigation items\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` line 46: `tabIndex={index === activeIndex ? 0 : -1}`.\n\n**The problem:** Only the active tab has `tabIndex={0}`. All other tabs have `tabIndex={-1}`. This means:\n- A keyboard user can Tab to the **active** tab only.\n- To reach other tabs, they must use `ArrowLeft`/`ArrowRight`.\n- If the user doesn't know arrow keys work (no visual or programmatic indication), they **cannot reach the other tabs via Tab**.\n\nThis is correct for the Tabs pattern (where arrow keys are the primary navigation method). But for navigation, all items should be in the tab order. A user should be able to Tab through all four navigation items without knowing about arrow keys.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) \u2014 all functionality must be operable via keyboard. WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order) \u2014 tab order must be logical. The roving tabindex creates a situation where keyboard access to 75% of the navigation items depends on knowledge of an undocumented keyboard shortcut.\n\n**Fix:** Remove roving tabindex. All navigation items should have default `tabIndex` (0). If arrow key navigation is kept as an enhancement, it should be additive, not the only way to reach inactive items.\n\n```jsx\n// Remove: tabIndex={index === activeIndex ? 0 : -1}\n// All <a> elements are naturally tabbable\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** A keyboard user who doesn't know about arrow keys can only reach the active tab. The other three tabs are invisible to Tab navigation. This is a keyboard trap of a different kind \u2014 not a trap that prevents escape, but a trap that prevents access.\n\n---\n\n### M4. No `<nav>` landmark\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` line 39: `<div className=\"product-nav-container\">`. The navigation container is a `<div>`, not a `<nav>` element.\n\n**User impact:**\n- **Screen reader users:** Landmark navigation (JAWS `B`, NVDA `D`) will not find this navigation. The user must navigate by links, tabs, or Tab order to find it.\n- **All AT users:** The navigation has no semantic landmark role. It is invisible to the page's structural outline.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 navigation content should be in a navigation landmark. WCAG 2.4.1 (Bypass Blocks) \u2014 users should be able to skip to main content, which requires a `<main>` landmark and a `<nav>` landmark.\n\n**Fix:** Wrap the navigation in a `<nav>` element:\n\n```jsx\n<nav aria-label=\"Product sections\">\n  <ul className=\"product-nav-list\">\n    {/* ... */}\n  </ul>\n</nav>\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** Without a `<nav>` landmark, this navigation is invisible to the most common screen reader navigation strategy (landmark navigation). The fix is a one-line change.\n\n---\n\n### M5. No content change announcement\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` \u2014 no `aria-live`, `aria-busy`, `role=\"status\"`, or `role=\"alert\"` anywhere in the component. The `role=\"tabpanel\"` div (line 57) has no live region role.\n\n**The problem:** When the route changes and new content loads, there is no programmatic announcement. A screen reader user who activates a tab (or arrow-navigates) gets no feedback that the content has changed. They must manually navigate to the content area to discover the change.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages) \u2014 status messages must be programmatically identifiable. WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 the relationship between the navigation action and the content change must be communicated.\n\n**Fix:** Add an `aria-live` region or use focus management (see M2) to announce the content change:\n\n```jsx\n<div aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"sr-only\">\n  {pathname && `Now viewing: ${sections.find(s => pathname.startsWith(s.path))?.label || ''}`}\n</div>\n```\n\nOr, more robustly, rely on focus management (M2) to move focus to the new content heading, which inherently announces the change.\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** Screen reader users get no feedback when the content changes. They must manually explore to discover the new content. This is a significant communication gap.\n\n---\n\n### M6. `role=\"tabpanel\"` wrapping full page content\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` lines 57\u201364:\n```jsx\n<div\n  role=\"tabpanel\"\n  id={`panel-${sections[activeIndex >= 0 ? activeIndex : 0].id}`}\n  aria-labelledby={`tab-${sections[activeIndex >= 0 ? activeIndex : 0].id}`}\n  tabIndex={0}\n  className=\"product-content\"\n>\n  {/* Page content rendered by Next.js router */}\n</div>\n```\n\n**The problem:** The content area is a full page with its own headings, landmarks, and structure. Wrapping it in `role=\"tabpanel\"` is semantically incorrect. A tab panel is a contained view within a tab widget. A page is a document. The `aria-labelledby` reference to the tab is also wrong \u2014 the page content is not \"labeled by\" the tab; it is a distinct page.\n\n**User impact:**\n- **Screen reader users:** The content is announced as a \"tab panel\" rather than a page. The heading structure within the content is subordinated to the tab panel role. The page's own landmarks (if any) are nested inside a tab panel, which is semantically incoherent.\n- **All AT users:** The `aria-labelledby` reference creates a false relationship between the tab and the page content.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 the semantic structure should match the actual content relationship. A page is not a tab panel.\n\n**Fix:** Remove `role=\"tabpanel\"`, `aria-labelledby`, and the `tabIndex={0}`. The content area is simply the page content:\n\n```jsx\n<main className=\"product-content\">\n  {/* Page content rendered by Next.js router */}\n</main>\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** The semantic structure is incoherent. A page is not a tab panel. The fix is to remove the tab panel wrapper and use `<main>` instead.\n\n---\n\n### M7. `aria-selected` used where `aria-current=\"page\"` is correct\n\n**Evidence:** `ProductNavigation.jsx` line 45: `aria-selected={index === activeIndex}`.\n\n**The problem:** `aria-selected` is for selection widgets (tabs, listbox options, tree items). It communicates \"this item is selected in a set.\" For navigation, the correct attribute is `aria-current=\"page\"`, which communicates \"this is the current page.\"\n\n**User impact:**\n- **Screen reader users:** The active item is announced as \"selected\" rather than \"current page.\" The semantic meaning is different. A screen reader user who understands `aria-current` will not get the expected \"current page\" announcement.\n\n**WCAG citation:** WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) \u2014 the state must be programmatically determinable and semantically correct.\n\n**Fix:** Replace `aria-selected` with `aria-current`:\n\n```jsx\n<a\n  href={section.path}\n  aria-current={pathname.startsWith(section.path) ? 'page' : undefined}\n>\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Why this matters:** The wrong ARIA attribute communicates the wrong semantic meaning. The fix is a one-attribute change.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n- **No skip link:** There is no \"Skip to main content\" link. A keyboard user must Tab through all four navigation items to reach the content. Add a visually-hidden-until-focused skip link before the navigation. WCAG 2.4.1 (Bypass Blocks).\n\n- **Focus indicator uses `outline-offset: -2px`:** The focus outline is drawn 2px inside the element boundary. Combined with the `border-bottom: 3px solid transparent` on the tab and the `border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb` on the tablist, the bottom of the focus indicator may be partially obscured. Consider `outline-offset: 2px` (outside) for better visibility. WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible), 2.4.13 (Focus Appearance). The 2px width and ~3.6:1 contrast meet the minimum, but the inward offset reduces practical visibility.\n\n- **No list semantics for navigation items:** The tabs are in a flat `<div>` with no `<ul>/<li>` structure. Navigation items should be in a list. WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships).\n\n## Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n- **No keyboard shortcut documentation:** Arrow keys are used for navigation but are not documented. A visually-hidden `<kbd>` hint or a \"Keyboard shortcuts\" section would help users discover this. (This becomes less relevant if the Tabs pattern is removed in favor of navigation semantics, where arrow keys are optional.)\n\n- **No `prefers-reduced-motion` media query:** No animations are present, so this is not applicable. If transitions are added in the future, respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n- **No `aria-hidden` on decorative elements:** No decorative elements are present. If icons are added, ensure they are `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps, Unhandled Edge Cases)\n\n1. **No `<nav>` landmark** \u2014 the navigation is invisible to landmark-based screen reader navigation.\n2. **No `<main>` landmark** \u2014 the content area is a `<div>`, not a `<main>`. Screen reader users cannot navigate to \"main content\" via landmarks.\n3. **No focus management after route change** \u2014 every navigation action results in focus loss.\n4. **No content change announcement** \u2014 screen reader users get no feedback when the content changes.\n5. **No skip link** \u2014 keyboard users must Tab through all navigation items to reach content.\n6. **No list semantics** \u2014 navigation items are not in a `<ul>/<li>` structure.\n7. **No `aria-current=\"page\"`** \u2014 the active navigation item is not marked as the current page.\n8. **No `lang` attribute visible** \u2014 not in this component, but should be on `<html>`. (Note: this is a page-level concern, not a component concern.)\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n**Screen reader user:** This is the most severely impacted user group. The component fails on three independent navigation strategies: (1) link navigation \u2014 items are buttons, not links; (2) landmark navigation \u2014 no `<nav>` element; (3) tab navigation \u2014 the pattern is misapplied, `aria-controls` references are broken, and the interaction model is wrong. A screen reader user who has memorized \"press K for links, B for landmarks, G for tabs\" will find this component through none of those strategies. The only reliable path is Tab order, and even that is limited by roving tabindex to the active item only.\n\n**Keyboard-only user:** Functional but degraded. The active tab is reachable via Tab. Other tabs require arrow keys (undocumented). After any navigation action, focus is lost and the user must Tab from the top of the page. Buttons don't support middle-click or right-click \u2192 open in new tab. No skip link.\n\n**Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast):** Contrast ratios are adequate (active tab ~6.4:1, inactive ~4.7:1, focus indicator ~3.6:1). Touch targets are ~43px tall, meeting the 24x24 minimum. The `white-space: nowrap` on tabs could cause horizontal scroll at 200% zoom if the container is narrow. The `outline-offset: -2px` reduces focus indicator visibility slightly. No `prefers-reduced-motion` needed (no animations).\n\n**Cognitive accessibility user:** The tab metaphor is clear and understandable. The interaction model is consistent (arrow keys work the same way on every tab). No timeouts, no destructive actions. However, the fact that these are buttons (not links) means users can't use standard link interactions (middle-click, right-click), which is a cognitive expectation mismatch. The lack of focus management after navigation is confusing \u2014 the user clicks a tab, the page changes, and then... nothing. Where am I?\n\n**Vestibular & motion:** No animations, transitions, or auto-playing content. LOW alarm.\n\n**Auditory access:** No media elements. LOW alarm.\n\n**Environmental contrast:** Color is not the sole indicator of active state (border-bottom and font-weight also change). Adequate contrast ratios. LOW alarm.\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Wrong ARIA pattern, broken aria-controls, no nav landmark, no link semantics, no content announcement |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Roving tabindex, focus loss after navigation, buttons instead of links, no skip link |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Focus indicator offset, potential horizontal scroll at 200% zoom |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Focus loss after navigation is confusing, buttons vs. links expectation mismatch |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Adequate contrast, color not sole indicator |\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT.** The component applies the WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern to page-level route navigation. This is not a partial implementation or a missing attribute \u2014 it is the wrong pattern for the interaction model. The APG Tabs pattern is for in-page view switching; this component navigates between distinct URLs with browser history entries. The result is a component that:\n\n1. Is invisible to screen reader users navigating by links (buttons, not links) or landmarks (no `<nav>`).\n2. Has 75% of its `aria-controls` references pointing to non-existent elements.\n3. Loses focus after every navigation action.\n4. Prevents Tab access to 75% of navigation items (roving tabindex).\n5. Wraps full page content in a `role=\"tabpanel\"` that semantically subordinates the page to a tab widget.\n\nThe \"Accessibility Features Present\" checklist is a false positive. Every attribute is there, but they describe a pattern that doesn't match the interaction. This is the classic \"80% implemented\" trap \u2014 except here, the 80% is the wrong 80%.\n\n**What would change the verdict to ACCEPT:** Replace the Tabs pattern with navigation semantics (`<nav>`, `<ul>/<li>`, `<a href>`, `aria-current=\"page\"`), add focus management after route change, add a skip link, and add a content change announcement. The arrow key navigation can be kept as an enhancement on top of the link semantics, but it must not be the only way to reach inactive items.\n\n**Recalibration notes:** I initially considered downgrading C1 (wrong pattern) to MAJOR because the items are technically accessible via Tab and arrow keys. However, the Realist Check confirmed: the realistic worst case is a screen reader user who navigates by links or landmarks and **cannot find this navigation at all**. That is a complete access failure for a core navigation component. CRITICAL is correct.\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. **Is this component intended to be a tab widget or navigation?** The design rationale argues for tabs, but the behavior (distinct URLs, browser history, back/forward) is navigation. If the intent is truly tabs, the implementation should use hash-based routing (`/product#specs`) instead of distinct URLs, and all four panels should be rendered in the DOM with visibility toggling. If the intent is navigation, the Tabs pattern should be removed. This is a design decision that must be resolved before implementation.\n\n2. **Does the page content have its own heading structure and landmarks?** The `role=\"tabpanel\"` wrapper (M6) may be masking a missing `<main>` or `<h1>` in the page content. If the page content is rendered by a separate component, its semantic structure should be reviewed independently.\n\n3. **Is there a global skip link or skip-to-content mechanism elsewhere in the app?** If so, the missing skip link in this component is less severe. If not, it is a page-level gap.\n\n4. **Are there other components in the app that use the same \"tabs for navigation\" pattern?** If this is a design system pattern, the fix should be applied at the system level, not per-component.",
